course: dictionary
lesson: 13531
title: Words with two meanings in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can mean different things, and the context tells you which meaning is right. «The vet implanted a tiny microchip under the cat's skin so it could be found if it got lost.» Implanted means put inside the body, usually by an operation, so the words under the skin are your clue.
explain: Now read this: «The story's message was implanted in my mind, and I thought about it for weeks.» Here nothing goes into a body. The words in my mind tell you that implanted means fixed firmly in someone's thoughts.
explain: Always check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try "put inside the body" or "fixed firmly" in place of implanted. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Implanted is the past form of the verb implant, so it means was put in or has been put in.
fact: Implanted can also be an adjective, as in «an implanted pacemaker».
fact: In medicine, a pacemaker can be implanted to help the heart keep a steady beat.
fact: In biology, a fertilised egg becomes implanted, or attached firmly, in the womb.
fact: Ideas can be implanted in a mind too, for example by a powerful story.
exercise: «The doctor implanted a tiny pacemaker to help her heart keep a steady beat.» What does implanted mean? | Taken out of the body | Washed carefully | Painted on the skin | *Put inside the body by surgery
exercise: «The story implanted a strong idea in my mind.» What does implanted suggest here? | Written on paper | Read aloud twice | Forgotten quickly | *Fixed firmly in the mind
exercise: Find and copy the past form of implant in this sentence: «The vet implanted a microchip in the dog.» | vet | dog | microchip | *implanted
test: Which of these is an example of something implanted in the body? | A pencil left on a desk | *A pacemaker placed inside a patient's chest by surgery | A kite flying in the wind | Coats hung on hooks
test: Nadia says a story «implanted a hope in her». What does this mean? | Made into a film | *Fixed firmly in her mind | Taken from her bag by a friend | Read out loud by her teacher
exam: «The fertilised egg became implanted in the womb.» In biology, what does implanted mean here? | Washed away by water | *Fixed firmly into place in the womb | Moved into another room | Eaten by the body
